<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Commius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Commius was a king of the Atrebates of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in the 1st century BCE. Installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, he served as Caesar's envoy to the Britons in 55 BCE, but joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a>'s revolt in 52 BCE and later fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Coins suggest he founded a dynasty among the British Atrebates, whose later kings, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verica">Verica</a>, claimed descent from him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/commius.jpg" alt="Commius" width="320" height="150" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: All rights reserved, David Lange, 2017-09-30 16:09:20 · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_Iron_Age_gold_plated_bronze_cored_stater_of_Commius_of_the_Atrebates_and_Regni._(FindID_867466).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Caesar recounts in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commentarii-de-bello-gallico">Commentarii de Bello Gallico</a> that he made Commius king of the Atrebates after conquering the tribe in 57 BCE. Sent ahead to persuade the Britons not to resist the first expedition, Commius was arrested as soon as he landed and was handed back only when the Britons failed to stop the Roman landing. He then supplied a small troop of cavalry, and on the second expedition he negotiated the surrender of the British leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassivellaunus">Cassivellaunus</a>.</p><p>He stayed loyal through the Gaulish revolts of 54 BCE, and Caesar in return left the Atrebates independent and free of tax and gave Commius rule over the Morini. According to Aulus Hirtius, who wrote the last book of the De Bello Gallico, the legate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-labienus">Titus Labienus</a> suspected him of conspiring with other tribes and sent the tribune Gaius Volusenus Quadratus to kill him at a sham meeting. Commius escaped with a severe head wound and vowed never again to associate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alesia-city">Alesia</a> he joined a revolt of the Bellovaci, bringing some 500 Germans to their side. In 51 BCE he waged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war with a small mounted band until Volusenus, sent after him by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>, beat his horsemen. Commius sued for peace, offering hostages on condition that he never had to meet a Roman again, and Antony agreed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sextus-julius-frontinus">Sextus Julius Frontinus</a>, writing in the 1st century CE, tells how Commius escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> with Caesar in pursuit, raising his sails on ships stranded by the tide so that Caesar thought them afloat. John Creighton argues instead that Commius was sent to Britain under his truce with Antony and set up there as a friendly king. Coins in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> pairing his name with Garmanos or Carsicios suggest he kept some power there, perhaps through regents or sons.</p><p>By about 30 BCE he was king of the Atrebates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, issuing coins from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calleva-atrebatum">Calleva Atrebatum</a> (Silchester). Coins with his name continued until about 20 BCE, and some have suggested two kings of the name, father and son, pointing to coins stamped COM COMMIOS. From about 25 BCE he seems to have ruled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tincomarus">Tincomarus</a>, whose coins, like those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eppillus">Eppillus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verica">Verica</a>, name him as a son of Commius.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Commius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After their defeat Caesar made <b>Commius</b> their king and used him as an envoy to the Britons in 55 BCE, extending his authority over the Morini.</blockquote><a href="/state/atrebates">Atrebates</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Commius</b>, king of the Atrebates of northern Gaul, first served Julius Caesar and then joined the Gallic revolt of 52 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/atrebates-britain">Atrebates (Britain)</a></li><li><blockquote>Before the conquest the region of Hampshire and Wiltshire seems to have fallen within the sphere of the Atrebates (Britain) and their kings <b>Commius</b>, Tincomarus and Verica, sharing their coinage and links with Gaul.</blockquote><a href="/state/belgae-britain">Belgae (Britain)</a></li></ul></section>
